#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124162 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "526b792b-9952-5dae-bcd6-15564c0e013f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3131" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,\\pi)$ given that $f(x,y) = (x - y)\\cos y.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the gradient at the specified point, we substitute $(x,y)= (1,\\pi)$ into the above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(1,\\pi ) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +(1-\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124163 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfade25e-065a-5c66-a554-9ee4e9559a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3132" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla g(x,y,z)$   at $(2,-1,3)$ if $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + xyz.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =2x+yz \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xz \\\\  & =xz \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xy \\\\  & =xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(2x+yz)\\text{ }i+(xz)\\text{ }j+(xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,3) & =(2(2)+(-1)(3))\\text{ }i+2(3)\\text{ }j+2(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =1\\text{ }i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124164 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2549d7b-07c2-5f2f-89a3-2cccecf2ba22" :question/math-academy-id "e-3133" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y-4)\\ln (x-2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y-4}{x-2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\  \\mathbf i + 0\\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{x-2}=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y-4=0,$ which means $y=4.$  From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x-2)=0,$ which means that $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf  0$ at the point $(3,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124166 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67e9bcb5-4bf2-56b8-8d82-95355841eaaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3150" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y) g(x,y)$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\sqrt{xy},\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla [fg] = g \\, \\nabla\\! f  + f \\, \\nabla\\! g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule shown above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+xy(\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f992c27a-3aa3-5b25-a53b-b09803eb38c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322277} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e6eaeb-666a-51e9-b07f-636cdbe0eb89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322278} {:answer/id #uuid "bbfa2fc3-370f-5074-b45d-a4bcfe22385c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322279} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf3d895-38fb-560e-9311-c84c978b5e70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322280} {:answer/id #uuid "ff4c28ca-2ae4-5148-873e-52fe0628f333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cf3d895-38fb-560e-9311-c84c978b5e70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322280} :answer-field/id #uuid "1efb1ae1-3f4a-5d3d-a89e-d5688befc68f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0611e4b-e342-5f93-a418-5af6bc5c2cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-127650" :question/problem "If $h(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}),$ find $∇h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea33666f-7eb0-5757-b93c-a4ca11a35aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322284} {:answer/id #uuid "4c19b6e4-bbb2-54cc-870f-18a106672097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322285} {:answer/id #uuid "f64ba2d6-bb9a-5174-8995-2ebca4eda0aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322286} {:answer/id #uuid "91d6ae38-a7d5-58eb-820c-5bc2594403ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322287} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7810ac-87fa-5a1c-87c8-b22074698773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7810ac-87fa-5a1c-87c8-b22074698773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322288} :answer-field/id #uuid "c77876dc-7325-58b6-8a70-d5e9e6af427c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3ab4912-f54b-5de4-a5e6-763ea328cbb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-128517" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0 \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ at two points: $(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87c9efef-7b68-5d9c-8468-dc3d8ec73f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,3)$ and $(-3,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322291} {:answer/id #uuid "130637e6-ae8d-5642-8d4d-d27f8095fe93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322292} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7872d8-997c-51aa-a689-d82426760f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-3)$ and $(-3,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322293} {:answer/id #uuid "e244813d-1802-5156-b946-a3e658a3fb3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322294} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf450eb-5364-5d7a-bf3e-3cd0ba197cc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "130637e6-ae8d-5642-8d4d-d27f8095fe93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322292} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0008cb5-9786-5e3d-be32-076d830d54ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a633d445-62c8-5864-b7bf-d694cd1cd56c" :question/math-academy-id "q-128519" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x,$ find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇g(x,y)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y)=(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{y})j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j=0\\,i+0\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1=0 \\\\ \\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ 9\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 81+3{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-27 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{3}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point: $(-3,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb402be-29c7-5e60-8660-43b00271b91a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(x(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+xy(1-{x}^{2})\\text{ }j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322298} {:answer/id #uuid "903e52db-bd77-5a8f-ae34-98f58f214a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({x}^{2}(1+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+2{y}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})\\text{ }j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322299} {:answer/id #uuid "3d72fcb5-7be4-5c2c-aac6-6de935f0babb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(y(1-2{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+x(1+2{y}^{2})\\text{ }j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322300} {:answer/id #uuid "277c2a61-d00e-5ff5-9275-715217b99986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}(1-{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+2xy(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322301} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8c3adb-4fe2-503b-907e-15c026b52154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}(1-2{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+2xy(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f8c3adb-4fe2-503b-907e-15c026b52154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}(1-2{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+2xy(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322302} :answer-field/id #uuid "b57d9015-1621-55da-b807-45484c16c305" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "213dbaa5-b611-5d26-80d0-68b5b3647c86" :question/math-academy-id "q-128521" :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $g(x,y)={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=f∇g+g∇f.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})={y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})=2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={y}^{2}\\text{ }i+2xy\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\text{ }j=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }i-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =x{y}^{2}(-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }i-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }j)+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }i+2xy\\text{ }j) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }i-2x{y}^{3}\\text{ }j)+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }i+2xy\\text{ }j) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}(1-2{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+2xy(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f93687f8-bf98-5b5d-91f7-2be6674a2c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}\\,j+2yz\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322305} {:answer/id #uuid "c55bf03e-9463-5db3-8051-83717315dc8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\,i-{z}^{2}\\,j-2yz\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322306} {:answer/id #uuid "0f6114bc-7474-51a6-8ee2-ca082c6c9ca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j-2xy\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322307} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5832ec-4186-5e11-af39-2dba80d6854d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}\\,i+3xy\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322308} {:answer/id #uuid "adc13558-be23-5d6e-b774-52c89b2b56cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\,i-2xz\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93687f8-bf98-5b5d-91f7-2be6674a2c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}\\,j+2yz\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322305} :answer-field/id #uuid "6141f78b-7288-58df-9f96-1af3e6ea9459" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad35730f-58dd-5654-94ad-54ae938df086" :question/math-academy-id "q-128529" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\, &  & ∇f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,i+2xy{z}^{3}\\,j+3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,k, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y,z) & =\\frac{1}{xyz},\\, &  & ∇h(x,y,z)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}z}\\,j-\\frac{1}{xy{z}^{2}}\\text{ }k. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h∇f+f∇h.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z)] & =h(x,y,z)∇f(x,y,z)+f(x,y,z)∇h(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xyz}({y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,i+2xy{z}^{3}\\,j+3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,k)+x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}z}\\,j-\\frac{1}{xy{z}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\,i+\\frac{2xy{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\,j+\\frac{3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{xyz}\\,k-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}z}\\,j-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xy{z}^{2}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\,i+2{z}^{2}\\,j+3yz\\,k-\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\,i-{z}^{2}\\,j-yz\\,k \\\\  & ={z}^{2}\\,j+2yz\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96c73ec0-eefd-50b0-adc5-09a051ddb3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122183} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d7c01e-d6d0-54ca-99b3-3d5aed419d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122184} {:answer/id #uuid "0a14965a-37a7-5094-b356-9daa506a937e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{6}, \\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122185} {:answer/id #uuid "4042808f-9ca0-57bd-ae30-cbef5a8872fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{6}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122186} {:answer/id #uuid "76d7759c-0ceb-5096-83c5-0c4e8a743b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4042808f-9ca0-57bd-ae30-cbef5a8872fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{6}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122186} :answer-field/id #uuid "a40c9e35-d8e0-5053-81c7-0c404abc082e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da858976-fa9b-559a-a71d-31223d9b1ce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-35995" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x) \\\\  & =6x+0-1 \\\\  & =6x-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x) \\\\  & =0+2y-0 \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=(6x-1)\\,i+2y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(6x-1)\\,i+2y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x-1=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\frac{1}{6}.$ From the second equation, we obtain $y=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{6},0)\\,.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c68294b-0fbd-533a-b28a-ade02f6f1473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322312} {:answer/id #uuid "a4df7eb0-3c26-5b38-8b3c-8c99d60ff686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\pi ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322313} {:answer/id #uuid "75ad26b6-4b71-5314-9b40-4d4081baa138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\pi ,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322314} {:answer/id #uuid "a19a28dc-5f3f-5716-a0c0-f918a1e503aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322315} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea2bb53-d001-51e3-ba3e-4fb89335aff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75ad26b6-4b71-5314-9b40-4d4081baa138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\pi ,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322314} :answer-field/id #uuid "db26f052-1bd9-5295-a69e-2c88308e514a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daba266b-1065-59e3-9dc2-9f65876ed44e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36005" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y)$ at $(1,1)$ if $h(x,y)=4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y,\\,\\frac{4x}{{y}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,1) & =⟨4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1,\\,\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{4}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi ,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "370101e8-f0d3-542f-88e1-85fb406467aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322319} {:answer/id #uuid "9a54122b-ae0b-527c-aa09-c75bc5d6cb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322320} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab86f77-0e51-5721-a100-0ce32d596b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322321} {:answer/id #uuid "83e8c5d6-1572-5aa3-a073-f1b3dd635640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322322} {:answer/id #uuid "f16f9c53-e38f-5977-bc6f-e09308a2dfcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "370101e8-f0d3-542f-88e1-85fb406467aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322319} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdd82221-210d-5d2e-ab3e-6dc5bedcecda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e4ad275-9622-528e-a709-a49a6640457d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36009" :question/problem "Evaluate the gradient of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ if $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),\\,{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟨x,\\,y⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\,0) & =\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{0}^{2}}⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\,\\frac{1}{4}}⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4883017-f52e-5143-88f1-06f5fc6774cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322325} {:answer/id #uuid "39db5d60-d92c-55d6-ad5d-42b522c4f0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+({x}^{4}y-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322326} {:answer/id #uuid "68fbaa21-de06-5eba-9ed9-f80797ea21a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16x{y}^{3}\\text{ }i+12{x}^{4}y\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322327} {:answer/id #uuid "c4147ce9-d5f1-5845-a6d8-e5912eca0598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+(12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322328} {:answer/id #uuid "e5afa44f-3cf2-5cca-bb12-f44fc4067b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3}+12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4147ce9-d5f1-5845-a6d8-e5912eca0598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+(12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322328} :answer-field/id #uuid "201f8647-3957-5bfa-8c43-e5fa35f20116" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7958e50f-73b4-5252-b8f0-e891d3332089" :question/math-academy-id "q-36144" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $g(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x,y) & =3{x}^{2}-4y,\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=6x\\text{ }i-4\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & =2{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\, &  & ∇h(x,y)=4x{y}^{2}\\text{ }i+4{x}^{2}y\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $g(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[gh]=h\\,∇\\,g+g\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[g(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)∇g(x,y)+g(x,y)∇h(x,y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(6x\\text{ }i-4\\text{ }j)+(3{x}^{2}-4y)(4x{y}^{2}\\text{ }i+4{x}^{2}y\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot 6x\\text{ }i-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot 4\\text{ }j)+((3{x}^{2}-4y)\\cdot 4x{y}^{2}\\text{ }i+(3{x}^{2}-4y)\\cdot 4{x}^{2}y\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\text{ }i-8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }j+(12{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+(12{x}^{4}y-16{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+(12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c29d9a-b56e-575a-bea6-fc9c720b4614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨16,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322332} {:answer/id #uuid "5119b1d7-d13e-59d0-abb9-2172b807cfd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322333} {:answer/id #uuid "51d7f3a3-0460-5db5-9724-4364d5b0f5b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322334} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4c2e76-f516-516d-86b4-b638410d0ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322335} {:answer/id #uuid "1d47619d-3143-503d-8a2e-833171e1f019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d47619d-3143-503d-8a2e-833171e1f019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322336} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b9313d2-223a-5492-8acd-3451f9c60859" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a7ed09f-9f05-555b-9eaa-822f524c2066" :question/math-academy-id "q-36909" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇f(x,y)$ at $(2,1)$ if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\sqrt{y+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y+3}) \\\\  & =2x\\sqrt{y+3} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y+3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\sqrt{y+3},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y+3}}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =⟨2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{1+3},\\,\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{1+3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70e3141d-a5d0-5ca1-b683-0e024ec5eb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+2y(\\frac{1}{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322339} {:answer/id #uuid "9d35c10d-6a93-59d8-81d2-417749409903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y}+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322340} {:answer/id #uuid "2149c1ce-92ee-5f0f-a4fc-a149e0e2dd39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322341} {:answer/id #uuid "444b83e9-e15e-50d4-992b-e48405842f1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\text{ }i+(y-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322342} {:answer/id #uuid "105995d5-2226-5382-a9b5-99a70b28be11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2149c1ce-92ee-5f0f-a4fc-a149e0e2dd39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322341} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f0035b9-d683-5dd8-92bd-aaf233d562f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd2b62c5-efe3-5e02-b543-0d0ea8f6f7df" :question/math-academy-id "q-36910" :question/problem "Given  $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})$, find $∇g(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05244f5e-d6a4-5d76-a9c3-b11654af4906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{xyz}(\\frac{1}{yz}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{xz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{xz}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322345} {:answer/id #uuid "bde88c02-d3a0-5dc5-9b71-2721b766cd22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xyz}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322346} {:answer/id #uuid "874788d4-cd64-5c6d-9dcc-ef206db24cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{xyz}(x\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }j+z\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322347} {:answer/id #uuid "dee595db-c30f-57ee-b47b-2bcdaa74dabb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(x\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }j+z\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322348} {:answer/id #uuid "935d9087-8c8c-5b2e-9f13-ba5456bc0201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "935d9087-8c8c-5b2e-9f13-ba5456bc0201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322349} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeaa6590-a3d4-5880-a241-0086a1c1766d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4903eb08-1095-52c0-bd3a-a8d7c39455f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-36918" :question/problem "Given $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xyz}$, find $∇f(x,y,z).$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88c1b753-5d93-5622-b595-82b74974bb25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+2}}i+\\frac{1}{2yz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2yz}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322351} {:answer/id #uuid "9e559626-0a27-505f-9b7c-cec6ee09bf35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+2}}i+\\frac{1}{yz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{yz}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322352} {:answer/id #uuid "f294e183-0e86-5b5c-b08d-9f16e038a1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+2}}i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322353} {:answer/id #uuid "c4acf4ea-3f54-5b30-93bf-a530a1a83247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+2}}i+\\frac{1}{2y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322354} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f86e3d-689f-5ef6-a7fb-49a3147851ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+2}}i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4acf4ea-3f54-5b30-93bf-a530a1a83247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+2}}i+\\frac{1}{2y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322354} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7dfa95d-03b9-5399-9a38-0fd1b4249864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e287a6b3-141e-5735-ae69-360760a9ed55" :question/math-academy-id "q-36968" :question/problem "Find $∇g(x,y,z)$ given that  $g(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x+2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz}).$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x+2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+2}}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{yz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\cdot z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2y} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x+2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{yz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\cdot y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+2}}i+\\frac{1}{2y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2z}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f35ce1-3d41-5800-b80b-1f9d6b494ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322358} {:answer/id #uuid "ea537af5-5a44-548c-99a4-cec902923a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\text{ }j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322359} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa8b1c8-c1b5-561c-a34d-295da42aa0fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ }i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322360} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa04ad3-1965-5cdd-bd20-64eb74dfdd88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322361} {:answer/id #uuid "340200eb-1f6d-5a1a-a225-ae6a5ad50768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37f35ce1-3d41-5800-b80b-1f9d6b494ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322358} :answer-field/id #uuid "b80b74db-3b3b-5238-9a12-9241366f66ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1593ff0-2dc2-5070-ae82-476823174170" :question/math-academy-id "q-36969" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇f(x,y,z)$ at the point $(2,3,1)$ if $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot yz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot xz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot xy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2z}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f15daa0f-7ee7-5bff-8566-b7fbc8aee3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322364} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3fafdb-413f-5bbd-8e1b-c53a0c4c8be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322365} {:answer/id #uuid "77a09554-e46b-5085-baa3-4230769af1bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322366} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2e5360-f4d9-5168-9617-3368ab8b0113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and  $(-1,-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322367} {:answer/id #uuid "71d02e70-f471-50eb-bda7-b04421b8b548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71d02e70-f471-50eb-bda7-b04421b8b548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322368} :answer-field/id #uuid "63bc7267-add2-5a8f-8045-91812d551433" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "adae0b04-7ccf-59da-b863-ae9717fcfd97" :question/math-academy-id "q-36970" :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =y+{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =x+2xy+0 \\\\  & =x+2xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=(y+{y}^{2})\\,i+(x+2xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+{y}^{2})\\,i+(x+2xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y+{y}^{2}=0 \\\\ x+2xy=0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation can be written as $y(1+y)=0$, which gives $y=0$ or $y=-1.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2x(0) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2x(-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49612262-a49c-53dc-9ae3-81f481100d53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322371} {:answer/id #uuid "5babed5e-6b7a-5f98-901f-f3092e7b8cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322372} {:answer/id #uuid "0b797bb8-edd0-506e-863a-5018ef70f599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+2)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+2)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322373} {:answer/id #uuid "e76bec4f-9988-54ba-b2f8-96a89f222fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322374} {:answer/id #uuid "b24e6539-ab07-5dc2-aba3-19202ef8839c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e76bec4f-9988-54ba-b2f8-96a89f222fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322374} :answer-field/id #uuid "0593a353-e82c-53ea-97f5-5104a066f347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e113ad4-1218-5093-a986-f3389728ec7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65131" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+xy(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }i]+[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+x\\text{ }j] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c239c6b-37e1-5b8e-a57a-73307d8c8417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }j+xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322378} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d68e50-2da9-58c3-8a78-76a81f5f608c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(yz+zx)\\text{ }i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(yz+zx)\\text{ }j+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322379} {:answer/id #uuid "f614b845-cc57-56a2-affd-17b2f34d9efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }i+xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322380} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6a6797-d951-5cf8-90fb-3941f50a293c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }i+xy\\text{ }j+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322381} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd1c9a6-260d-55ee-8e92-f9bc072ea873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfd1c9a6-260d-55ee-8e92-f9bc072ea873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322382} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ea13f0c-3125-506e-9a94-48f0f0d33cd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c344ff7-e9ae-55ca-a2d0-77f8ef1490ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-71422" :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)$, find $∇h(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z) \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z) \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & ={h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{h}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{h}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d80a54-30e2-5bd0-8023-bf9d6698e46d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+yz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }i+(2y+xz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }j+(2z+xy{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322385} {:answer/id #uuid "e50247b8-673b-560e-b76c-e9399a671eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }j+(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322386} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f4f116-5750-5a06-a555-132903ae092a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz-1})\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz-1})\\text{ }j+({x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz-1})\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322387} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ada029-7762-5cae-960b-143b9714d9ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }j+(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322388} {:answer/id #uuid "d512f83e-f779-5173-bfbd-7a0bc07a64f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+x{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+y{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }j+(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+z{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4ada029-7762-5cae-960b-143b9714d9ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }j+(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322388} :answer-field/id #uuid "63fc4b39-b3ff-54be-8905-db2cb09ac40a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3f07e77-96e3-5bfa-9689-789d29542b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-71425" :question/problem "Find $∇g(x,y,z)$ given that  $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }j+(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12592be6-7f46-5867-a2d2-a69a61893d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322392} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab050ae-4758-54a4-98e5-e62bd3f6e0a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,-1)$ and $(-1,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322393} {:answer/id #uuid "8db1d618-7d19-58f8-9d65-696e12d1a3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322394} {:answer/id #uuid "d547e652-8772-56bc-8a8f-0825434af6b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322395} {:answer/id #uuid "81b787e3-156c-5422-a609-591ead68d542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,-1)$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8db1d618-7d19-58f8-9d65-696e12d1a3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322394} :answer-field/id #uuid "0077ef26-76dd-59ee-8585-1f32ea88d648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c503ae86-f71f-5a21-9c90-e19ce4d410a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71474" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z$, find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇f(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}+0-\\frac{1}{z{x}^{2}}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}+0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =0+0-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}x}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k.$ \n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-1.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}(-1)}{{z}^{2}(-1)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{(-1)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ z+y & =0 \\\\ y & =-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-1$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=1.$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d0f5df5-2f99-5690-9a46-2374409c79f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322399} {:answer/id #uuid "deb84c0b-281e-53a0-a7a0-bb21b6bf1f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322400} {:answer/id #uuid "b487e61e-b8bb-5c12-8157-0d84ead37113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322401} {:answer/id #uuid "21c6343b-66c7-5ef7-aacd-518ca263f002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322402} {:answer/id #uuid "0654547a-83a9-57a1-9ef6-25b3042b422d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deb84c0b-281e-53a0-a7a0-bb21b6bf1f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322400} :answer-field/id #uuid "97dee7e9-d318-5575-be9a-b1275f503278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da58e4f4-442d-5b36-8664-807bdb33a068" :question/math-academy-id "q-71476" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2},\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=(2xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+2xy)\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{xy},\\, &  & ∇h(x,y)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇h(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}((2xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+2xy)\\text{ }j)+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2xy+{y}^{2}}{xy}\\text{ }i+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy}\\text{ }j)+(-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }i-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{x+2y}{y}\\text{ }j)+(-\\frac{x+y}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{x+y}{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{x+y}{x}+\\frac{2x+y}{x})\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x+y}{y}+\\frac{x+2y}{y})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
